title: Reserved fields
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: reference
summary: mfr_* fields are the daemon's (writing one needs force); mf_* fields steer the daemon (free to write, but an unknown name is refused).

Two prefixes are reserved:

* `mfr_*` (''metafolder restricted'') — written by the daemon. A write of your own needs `force` (`--force`/`-f` on the command line), or is refused with `400`.
* `mf_*` (''metafolder'') — read by the daemon to decide what it does; written freely, without `force`. An `mf_*` name the daemon does not know is refused with `400`, so that a typo cannot silently do nothing.

A forced `mfr_*` value is not protected: the watcher and reconcile overwrite `mfr_*`
fields whenever they update them. `force` acknowledges exactly that. Its legitimate
uses: restoring permissions after a mistaken recursive change, correcting a
misdetected MIME type, settling an `mfr_path` conflict the watcher cannot.

```
{"name": "mfr_permissions", "value": {"type": "string", "value": "0755"}, "force": true}
```

!! The fields of a file

|!Field |!Type |!Holds |
|<<cmd "mfr_path">> |`tree_ref` |the position in the filesystem's tree ([[TreeRef path conventions]]) — see below |
|<<cmd "mfr_type">> |`string` |`file`, `dir` or `symlink` (a symlink is never followed) |
|<<cmd "mfr_size">> |`int` |the size in bytes at the last update; a symlink's is its target path's length |
|<<cmd "mfr_mtime">> |`datetime` |the last content modification (a symlink's own) |
|<<cmd "mfr_btime">> |`datetime` |the creation time, when the filesystem records one |
|<<cmd "mfr_symlink_target">> |`string` |a symlink's target path; only on symlinks |
|<<cmd "mfr_permissions">> |`string` |the Unix permission bits in octal, `"0644"`; Unix only |
|<<cmd "mfr_uid">>, <<cmd "mfr_gid">> |`int` |the owner's user and group ids; Unix only |
|<<cmd "mfr_inode">> |`string` |`<dev>:<ino>`, only on a file with more than one hard link — its presence marks a hard link ([[What counts as a duplicate]]); Unix only |
|<<cmd "mfr_mime">> |`string` |the MIME type from the content, computed by reconcile ([[Moves and candidates]]); absent when undetectable or not yet computed |
|<<cmd "mfr_partial_hash">> |`string` |xxHash3 of the first and last 4 KiB, hex; absent until computed, cleared by a modification |
|<<cmd "mfr_full_hash">> |`string` |xxHash3 of the whole content, hex |
|<<cmd "mfr_hash_mtime">>, <<cmd "mfr_hash_size">> |`datetime`, `int` |the file's mtime and size when its hashes were computed, written and cleared with them ([[The duplicate hash cache]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_duplicate_group">> |`ref` |the duplicate group the file belongs to ([[Duplicate groups]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_mount">> |`string` |on a directory that is a mount point: the mounted volume's identity, best effort; never cleared ([[mfr_mount]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_path_old">> |`string` |where an orphaned metarecord last lived, frozen when its `mfr_path` became `nothing` ([[Orphans]], [[Event semantics]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_watch_exceeded">> |`bool` |on a directory the watch budget could not cover, inherited like `mf_watch` ([[The watch budget]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_meta_*">> |any |a piece of embedded metadata (tags, EXIF…), by the repository's metadata map ([[Embedded metadata]]) |
|<<cmd "mfr_meta_extracted">> |`bool` |that the file's embedded metadata has been read |

!! The fields that steer the daemon

|!Field |!Type |!Holds |
|<<cmd "mf_watch">> |`bool` |whether this metarecord and what lies under it are tracked — inherited down `mfr_path`, off by default ([[Watch and ignore fields]]) |
|<<cmd "mf_ignore">> |`string` |an ignore pattern, one row per pattern ([[Ignore presets]]) |
|<<cmd "mf_schema">> |`string` |the metarecord's types, one row per type ([[Schema]]) |
|<<cmd "mf_sync">> |`string` |`internal` (default) or `external`: whether metafolder or another tool owns this content during sync; inherited down `mfr_path` |

A duplicate group is a metarecord the daemon creates with no file behind it
(`mf_schema = duplicate_group`), carrying <<cmd "mfr_content_hash">>, <<cmd "mfr_content_size">>,
<<cmd "mfr_duplicate_count">> and <<cmd "mfr_duplicate_reclaimable">> ([[Duplicate groups]]) — the one
place the daemon writes an `mf_*` field of its own accord.

!! The four states of mfr_path

* ''a position'' — the file is there;
* ''a stale position'' — a path that no longer exists on disk: the location is uncertain, and reconcile looks for the file (by fingerprint) and offers candidates;
* ''`nothing`'' — the file was seen removed: no search needed. Only the watcher (and a forced write) sets `mfr_path` to `nothing`; reconcile never does;
* ''no field'' — no path was ever assigned ([[Nothing versus unknown]]).
